{
  "about": "Who a call through a register may reach, for the stack bound of the fixed-memory images (scripts/lib/stack_bound_gate.py, docs/text-ownership.md). A register can hold only an address the image holds somewhere (a data word, a literal pool, a movw/movt pair, an adr), so a rule names, among those functions, the ones its site may call. Every call by register in a function the bound reaches needs a rule; there is no reading of the vtable slot or of the method named on the line. `where` is a source file (the end of its path) and line, or, for C, a regex on the function's name. `callees` is a regex on the demangled name without generic arguments, or WAKER (the four functions of the executor's RawWakerVTable, read from the image), or TRAIT_METHODS (every trait-impl method the image holds, for a site that is shallow), or NONE. A NONE rule states a premise, and `absent` (a regex on the functions the image holds an address of) or `absent_reached` (on the functions the bound reaches) is that premise: the gate fails when it stops being true. A rule is written by a person and read against the code; the gate holds it to the image.",
  "schema": 1,
  "cold": [
    "^core::str::slice_error_fail"
  ],
